PicoGKRuntime 26.2 (0f26321c) — bulk mesh transfer in the C ABI.

PROPOSAL, NOT APPLIED LOCALLY. picovoxel ships this as a sibling translation
unit (src/pico-bulk.cpp) so the vendored tree stays pristine; this file is the
same code shaped as a diff against upstream, for leap71/PicoGKRuntime. Ledger
row U4. Companion PR text: upstream/pr-bulk-mesh-transfer.md.

REPO SPLIT: this patch is the C ABI half only. The C# binding half —
Mesh.vVertices()/vTriangles() reading through the four new exports instead of
looping Mesh_GetVertex/Mesh_GetTriangle — targets a DIFFERENT repository
(leap71/PicoGK) and is described in the PR text rather than diffed here.

Why: the ABI moves mesh data one element per call. Reading a 174k-vertex mesh
costs ~522k crossings; on managed and wasm hosts the crossing dominates
everything else. Measured in the picovoxel WebAssembly port: 522k crossings
become 2, ~150x faster on readback. The same shape serves C#, Python and any
other binding.

Signatures follow the existing caller-supplied-buffer precedent
(Voxels_GetZSlice, PicoGK.h:341). Reads clamp to the caller's buffer and return
the count written; writes return the index of the first appended element, or -1
on bad input, and go through Mesh::nAddVertex/nAddTriangle so the bounding box
and nMemUsage bookkeeping stay correct.

Layout note: Vector3 and Triangle are 12-byte #pragma pack(1) types identical
in layout to PKVector3/PKTriangle. Vector3 has a user-provided copy constructor
(PicoGKTypes.h:146), so std::copy and not memcpy — clang lowers it to the same
vectorised move at -O3.

Differentially tested byte-identical against the per-element path (100k-vertex
FNV-1a comparison) in the picovoxel suite. Evidence: the R11 measurement in
MIGRATING-FROM-CSHARP.md U4; the shipped implementation in src/pico-bulk.cpp.

Applies with `patch -p1` to a pristine PicoGKRuntime 0f26321c tree.

